feat(ci): refuse to track a local-only specification

The eight numbered documents are local only, and the whole of that was one
.gitignore line with nothing behind it. Measured against the real repository:

  git add -f docs/07-...              stages a spec; gitignore does not apply
  comment out `docs/*`, git add -A    stages all thirty-four

Neither is exotic, and no existing gate caught either. no-operator-paths.sh
passed with a spec staged, because it asks whether a tracked file NAMES an
operator path — not whether a document that must never ship has become tracked.

The threat is not malice. It is an agent force-adding a directory to be helpful,
or "fixing" a gitignore that appeared to be hiding files. A threat model in a
public repository is a map of where to look, and it cannot be unpublished.

The gate refuses any tracked OR STAGED path under docs/ that is not under
docs/adr/ — staged as well as tracked, because a gate that only read the
committed tree would pass in the moment before the commit that publishes, which
is the moment someone is most likely to run it. docs/adr/ is the one directory
that ships, by the root .gitignore and by CLAUDE.md, and the self-test requires
adding an ADR to leave the gate green.

THE HOOK HALF WAS BUILT WRONG FIRST, AND THE ONLY REASON I KNOW IS THAT THE
PROBE FAILED. `install-hooks` copied into .git/hooks; this repository sets
core.hooksPath to scripts/hooks, so git never looked there. The hook was
silently dead, the commit that was supposed to be refused succeeded, and it
committed the THREAT MODEL onto main — reset, never pushed.

So the hook is now a TRACKED file in scripts/hooks, where git actually looks and
where it travels with a clone and with every worktree, and `install-hooks` ends
by asking `git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-commit` — where git will look,
honouring core.hooksPath — and FAILS if there is no executable hook there.
Reporting "installed" because a file was copied is precisely what let the spec
through.

The gate is the enforcement and the hook is only the fast answer: hooks are
local, and a subagent on a checkout that never ran install-hooks has none. CI
applies to whatever was actually pushed.

